<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The functor discipline ~ the record

The law reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/functor-discipline`>>. This record keeps how it arrived.

The operator named the discipline on 2026-06-29 and the house co-derived it; it seated as a lens at Synthesis, to earn Canon by talk-story over time.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/folds`>>

!! The folds ~ the collapses that ground the compose instance

The collapses ratified the discipline functorially: the four Python holders folded onto a cap-composition foundation and the palace became a cap-stack instance — each //composed// a shared transport while //preserving every op-surface//, the collapse landing byte-stable. Evidence: `git:b18235f6` (the holder collapse) and `git:0af5e326` (the palace collapse), both verified.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/enacted`>>

!! Enacted ~ the two sessions, as the meme told them

''Enacted once (this session).'' The recall query-derive moved off the coordinator into the in-VM
`@daemon` worker — the node-side derive and its disk basis-cache deleted, no fallback kept — so capture
and recall apply the ''identical'' functor against the same grammar/state. The derive that once ran two
places (and so risked two maps) runs one.

''Enacted again (this session).'' The worldline reads — the registry lookup and the compare/trajectory
derive — moved off the node coordinator into the in-VM worker: a node-side holder deleted, the reads
routed to the worker over `askIsland`. A carve-out had split a worldline READ onto the coordinator; the
correction ''reversed'' it and returned the read to the stack, so the cap-stack lifts whole once more.
The live proof of the rule it sharpens — no cap, not even a read,
stays behind.

<<~/ahu>>

